---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { site } from '../../data/site';
import ProjectDiagram from '../../components/diagram/ProjectDiagram.astro';
import yaaaCanvas from '../../diagrams/yaaa.canvas?raw';

const formulaLines = [
  'federated subsystems',
  '+ user-owned memory',
  '+ local-first routing',
  '+ gated action',
  '+ reviewed governance',
  '= a personal assistant ops architecture',
];

const stackRows = [
  { name: 'Sense', state: 'partial', label: 'partial · live' },
  { name: 'Remember', state: 'partial', label: 'substrate live' },
  { name: 'Reason', state: 'partial', label: 'partial · live' },
  { name: 'Converse', state: 'pending', label: 'pending' },
  { name: 'Act', state: 'partial', label: 'gate verified', gated: true },
  { name: 'Surface', state: 'live', label: 'live' },
];


const openQuestions = [
  'Which conversation harness earns the seat? Any candidate that creates a second write path or a second memory ledger is disqualified.',
  'How does memory binding land without a second ledger? Serving layers must stay rebuildable projections of the git-backed record.',
  'How is the high-sensitivity path kept safe? Local routing is the chosen design; leak detection guards any path toward a cloud model.',
  'Can self-improvement ever be on by default? Learning loops that mutate skills or memory without review are treated as fail-open, so they stay off or behind staged review.',
];
---

<Base
  title={`${site.name} · Yaaa`}
  description="Yaaa is a personal assistant ops architecture: a personal AI assistant assembled as a federation of small, auditable systems instead of a monolithic black box."
  ogType="article"
  wide
>
  <main class="yaaa-page">
    <section class="hero" aria-labelledby="project-title">
      <div class="hero-copy">
        <div class="badge-row" aria-label="Project domains">
          <span class="mode-tag">Systems</span>
          <span class="mode-tag mode-tag-secondary">Embodied Life</span>
        </div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Yet Another AI Assistant</p>
        <h1 id="project-title">an assistant you actually own</h1>
        <p class="summary">
          Yaaa is a personal assistant ops architecture — a self-hostable,
          local-first system where memory, routing, and every action stay
          versioned, reviewed, and owned by you. Sensitive work runs local, cloud
          use is bounded and gated, and conversation harnesses can be replaced
          without moving the source of truth.
        </p>
      </div>

      <figure
        class="subsystem-stack"
        role="img"
        aria-label="Diagram of the Yaaa subsystems on a shared binding bus: sense, remember, reason, converse, act (the write boundary), and surface"
      >
        <span class="stack-title">runtime loop</span>
        <ul>
          {stackRows.map((row) => (
            <li>
              <b>{row.name}</b>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
        <figcaption>A runtime loop of subsystems on a shared bus.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </section>

    <div class="spine-wrap">
      <section class="page-section" id="problem" aria-labelledby="problem-title">
        <div class="section-label">
          <span class="sec-no">01</span>
          <h2 id="problem-title">Problem</h2>
        </div>
        <p class="lead problem-intro">
          A personal assistant is the most concentrated data you will ever hand to
          one company. Consumer assistants optimize for convenience; the price is
          that your most intimate context ends up owned, screened, and locked
          inside a vendor.
        </p>
        <figure class="problem-figure" role="img" aria-label="Your personal data streams — mail, notes, calendar, camera, health, location, chats — converge into one commercial entity that aggregates and watches them; once sent, the data cannot be recalled.">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 560 262" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
            <defs>
              <marker id="pf-arr" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse">
                <path class="pf-arr-h" d="M0,0 L10,5 L0,10 z"/>
              </marker>
            </defs>
            <g aria-hidden="true">
              <line class="pf-line" x1="98" y1="27" x2="238" y2="130"/>
              <line class="pf-line" x1="98" y1="59" x2="238" y2="130"/>
              <line class="pf-line" x1="98" y1="91" x2="238" y2="130"/>
              <line class="pf-line" x1="98" y1="123" x2="238" y2="130"/>
              <line class="pf-line" x1="98" y1="155" x2="238" y2="130"/>
              <line class="pf-line" x1="98" y1="187" x2="238" y2="130"/>
              <line class="pf-line" x1="98" y1="219" x2="238" y2="130"/>
              <rect class="pf-chip" x="6" y="16" width="92" height="22" rx="4"/>
              <text class="pf-chip-t" x="52" y="31" text-anchor="middle">mail</text>
              <rect class="pf-chip" x="6" y="48" width="92" height="22" rx="4"/>
              <text class="pf-chip-t" x="52" y="63" text-anchor="middle">notes</text>
              <rect class="pf-chip" x="6" y="80" width="92" height="22" rx="4"/>
              <text class="pf-chip-t" x="52" y="95" text-anchor="middle">calendar</text>
              <rect class="pf-chip" x="6" y="112" width="92" height="22" rx="4"/>
              <text class="pf-chip-t" x="52" y="127" text-anchor="middle">camera</text>
              <rect class="pf-chip" x="6" y="144" width="92" height="22" rx="4"/>
              <text class="pf-chip-t" x="52" y="159" text-anchor="middle">health</text>
              <rect class="pf-chip" x="6" y="176" width="92" height="22" rx="4"/>
              <text class="pf-chip-t" x="52" y="191" text-anchor="middle">location</text>
              <rect class="pf-chip" x="6" y="208" width="92" height="22" rx="4"/>
              <text class="pf-chip-t" x="52" y="223" text-anchor="middle">chats</text>
              <text class="pf-faint" x="52" y="250" text-anchor="middle">your data streams</text>
              <rect class="pf-box" x="240" y="88" width="140" height="84" rx="6"/>
              <ellipse class="pf-eye" cx="310" cy="112" rx="14" ry="8"/>
              <circle class="pf-pupil" cx="310" cy="112" r="3.6"/>
              <text class="pf-box-t" x="310" y="145" text-anchor="middle">one commercial</text>
              <text class="pf-box-t" x="310" y="161" text-anchor="middle">entity</text>
              <line class="pf-out" x1="380" y1="124" x2="500" y2="124" marker-end="url(#pf-arr)"/>
              <text class="pf-faint" x="440" y="116" text-anchor="middle">sent out</text>
              <line class="pf-block" x1="500" y1="146" x2="388" y2="146"/>
              <path class="pf-x" d="M437 140 L449 152 M449 140 L437 152"/>
              <text class="pf-block-t" x="443" y="170" text-anchor="middle">no recall</text>
            </g>
          </svg>
          <figcaption>Everything in, nothing back.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <div class="problem-grid">
          <article>
            <span class="problem-label">Aggregated · unrecallable</span>
            <p>
              An assistant sees far more than email or a social feed — behavior,
              identity, legal and medical history, conversations, private notes,
              the cameras in your home, even the shape of your thinking, pooled
              inside one commercial entity. Once that context leaves, it cannot be
              taken back.
            </p>
          </article>
          <article>
            <span class="problem-label">Screened · surveilled</span>
            <p>
              Data routed to a cloud model is subject to that vendor's moderation,
              monitoring, and retention. Your assistant's inputs and outputs are
              watched on terms that are not yours.
            </p>
          </article>
          <article>
            <span class="problem-label">Locked · fragmented</span>
            <p>
              Each vendor keeps agent memory in its own format, and nothing syncs
              between them. Memory drifts across ChatGPT, Claude, and the next
              tool; you reconcile the gaps by hand — and human memory fails too. An
              assistant meant to lift that burden quietly adds to it.
            </p>
          </article>
        </div>
        <p class="problem-response">
          Yaaa starts from the opposite end: an assistant for you, on
          infrastructure you own, where memory is one reviewable record, sensitive
          work stays local, and cloud use is bounded and gated. Ownership is not a
          feature toggle. It lives in the architecture.
        </p>
        <figure class="problem-figure" role="img" aria-label="The same data streams gather into one store you own — your source of truth, on your own infrastructure — and leave only through a gate: sensitive data stays local, and only bounded, reviewed data goes out.">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 560 262" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
            <defs>
              <marker id="pr-arr" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path class="pr-arr-h" d="M0,0 L10,5 L0,10 z"/></marker>
            </defs>
            <g aria-hidden="true">
              <line class="pf-line" x1="98" y1="27" x2="238" y2="130"/>
              <line class="pf-line" x1="98" y1="59" x2="238" y2="130"/>
              <line class="pf-line" x1="98" y1="91" x2="238" y2="130"/>
              <line class="pf-line" x1="98" y1="123" x2="238" y2="130"/>
              <line class="pf-line" x1="98" y1="155" x2="238" y2="130"/>
              <line class="pf-line" x1="98" y1="187" x2="238" y2="130"/>
              <line class="pf-line" x1="98" y1="219" x2="238" y2="130"/>
              <rect class="pf-chip" x="6" y="16" width="92" height="22" rx="4"/>
              <text class="pf-chip-t" x="52" y="31" text-anchor="middle">mail</text>
              <rect class="pf-chip" x="6" y="48" width="92" height="22" rx="4"/>
              <text class="pf-chip-t" x="52" y="63" text-anchor="middle">notes</text>
              <rect class="pf-chip" x="6" y="80" width="92" height="22" rx="4"/>
              <text class="pf-chip-t" x="52" y="95" text-anchor="middle">calendar</text>
              <rect class="pf-chip" x="6" y="112" width="92" height="22" rx="4"/>
              <text class="pf-chip-t" x="52" y="127" text-anchor="middle">camera</text>
              <rect class="pf-chip" x="6" y="144" width="92" height="22" rx="4"/>
              <text class="pf-chip-t" x="52" y="159" text-anchor="middle">health</text>
              <rect class="pf-chip" x="6" y="176" width="92" height="22" rx="4"/>
              <text class="pf-chip-t" x="52" y="191" text-anchor="middle">location</text>
              <rect class="pf-chip" x="6" y="208" width="92" height="22" rx="4"/>
              <text class="pf-chip-t" x="52" y="223" text-anchor="middle">chats</text>
              <text class="pf-faint" x="52" y="250" text-anchor="middle">your data streams</text>
              <rect class="pr-box" x="240" y="88" width="140" height="84" rx="6"/>
              <path class="pr-glyph" d="M296 116 L310 103 L324 116 M300 116 L300 126 L320 126 L320 116"/>
              <text class="pr-box-t" x="310" y="147" text-anchor="middle">your source</text>
              <text class="pr-box-t" x="310" y="163" text-anchor="middle">of truth</text>
              <line class="pr-out" x1="380" y1="128" x2="444" y2="128"/>
              <rect class="pr-glyph" x="450" y="122" width="16" height="13" rx="2"/>
              <path class="pr-glyph" d="M453 122 v-4 a5 5 0 0 1 10 0 v4"/>
              <line class="pr-out-thin" x1="472" y1="120" x2="534" y2="104" marker-end="url(#pr-arr)"/>
              <text class="pr-label" x="512" y="96" text-anchor="middle">bounded out</text>
              <path class="pr-out-thin" d="M458 141 C458 180 402 180 356 172" marker-end="url(#pr-arr)"/>
              <text class="pr-label" x="404" y="194" text-anchor="middle">sensitive stays</text>
            </g>
          </svg>
          <figcaption>Yours by design — gathered, owned, and gated.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </section>

      <section class="page-section" id="concept" aria-labelledby="concept-title">
        <div class="section-label">
          <span class="sec-no">02</span>
          <h2 id="concept-title">Concept</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="concept-grid">
          <div>
            <p class="kicker">What it is</p>
            <h3>A binding architecture over federated subsystems.</h3>
            <p>
              Federated subsystems are the separate tools you already run — mail,
              notes, calendar, a smart-home hub — each with its own runtime and
              data. Rather than replace them with one central process, Yaaa binds
              them into a single assistant through memory, conversation, surface,
              and governance.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <p class="kicker">What it is not</p>
            <h3>Not a monolith, and not centralized.</h3>
            <p>
              No conversation harness owns memory, routing, or the write path.
              Yaaa coordinates subsystems through reviewed records and explicit
              gates; it does not absorb them into one black box.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div
          class="formula"
          role="img"
          aria-label="Architecture formula: federated subsystems, plus user-owned memory, plus local-first routing, plus gated action, plus reviewed governance, equals a personal assistant ops architecture"
        >
          {formulaLines.map((line) => <span>{line}</span>)}
        </div>
        <p class="formula-note">
          The design question is ownership, not feature coverage: where memory
          lives, which runtime may see sensitive context, and what must happen
          before an action leaves the system.
        </p>
        <p class="stance-note">
          The collaboration stance: you set the direction, the assistant manages
          the process — never the reverse. That boundary is architectural, not a
          matter of trust.
        </p>
        <div class="compare">
          <p class="kicker">Ops discipline, different subject</p>
          <div class="compare-grid">
            <article>
              <span class="compare-label">LLMOps</span>
              <p>
                Runs models in production for a team — serving, prompt and
                fine-tune pipelines, monitoring, evaluation, throughput, and cost.
                It optimizes how well an LLM service operates.
              </p>
            </article>
            <article>
              <span class="compare-label compare-label-accent">Yaaa</span>
              <p>
                Runs one assistant for one person — a reviewable memory of record,
                sensitivity-aware routing, gated actions, and portability across
                harnesses. It optimizes who owns and can audit the result.
              </p>
            </article>
          </div>
          <p class="compare-note">
            Same rigor — versioning, review, gates, audit — pointed at ownership,
            not throughput.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="page-section" id="how-it-works" aria-labelledby="loop-title">
        <div class="section-label">
          <span class="sec-no">03</span>
          <h2 id="loop-title">How it works</h2>
        </div>
        <p class="policy-note">
          The runtime loop above is what runs; the L0–L5 layers below are where
          authority sits. They overlap, but they are not a one-to-one mapping.
        </p>
        <figure class="arch-map">
          <ProjectDiagram
            canvasRaw={yaaaCanvas}
            accent="systems"
            title="Yaaa L0-L5 authority map: IO boundary, SENSE, binding layer, nested ReAct, gated action, and governance foundation"
          />
          <figcaption>The L0–L5 authority map: where each layer may read, decide, and write.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p class="policy-note">
          Each block carries a role chip — <b>ENTITY</b> data, <b>FLOW</b>
          transform, <b>ACTION</b> side effect, <b>CTRL</b> policy or gate. Read it
          top-to-bottom: input enters at L0, memory sits at the center, and side
          effects funnel down to L4.
        </p>
        <div class="layer-stack">
          <section class="layer-block wide L0" aria-label="L0 IO boundary">
            <h3 class="layer-head"><span class="layer-code">L0</span>IO boundary</h3>
            <div class="layer-body">
              <div class="layer-text">
                <p>L0 is the system's only contact with the outside world. Inputs enter and outputs leave, and each one crosses a single visible boundary — nothing reaches a model or leaves for a channel off the record.</p>
                <p>The four quadrants separate <em>how</em> an event flows. Passive input arrives on its own — feeds, timers, subscribed sources; proactive input is you — a prompt, reply, or approval. Passive output is a surface you pull when you choose; proactive output is the system reaching out. Keeping them distinct stops a quiet notification from posing as a pull surface, or an ambient feed from acting like your instruction.</p>
                <dl class="layer-legend">
                  <div><dt>passive input</dt><dd>environment, timers, subscribed sources</dd></div>
                  <div><dt>proactive input</dt><dd>your prompts, replies, approvals</dd></div>
                  <div><dt>passive output</dt><dd>pull surfaces you check — inbox, trace view</dd></div>
                  <div><dt>proactive output</dt><dd>pushes — asks, alerts, results</dd></div>
                </dl>
              </div>
              <figure class="layer-mini">
                <svg viewBox="0 0 540 108" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
                  <defs><marker id="mL0" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto"><path class="m-h" d="M0,0 L10,5 L0,10 z"/></marker></defs>
                  <rect class="m-box" x="6" y="6" width="150" height="40" rx="6"/><text class="m-t" x="81" y="30" text-anchor="middle">passive input</text>
                  <rect class="m-box" x="6" y="60" width="150" height="40" rx="6"/><text class="m-t" x="81" y="84" text-anchor="middle">proactive input</text>
                  <rect class="m-box" x="384" y="6" width="150" height="40" rx="6"/><text class="m-t" x="459" y="30" text-anchor="middle">passive output</text>
                  <rect class="m-box" x="384" y="60" width="150" height="40" rx="6"/><text class="m-t" x="459" y="84" text-anchor="middle">proactive output</text>
                  <line class="m-line m-proj" x1="270" y1="4" x2="270" y2="104"/>
                  <line class="m-line" x1="156" y1="26" x2="264" y2="26" marker-end="url(#mL0)"/>
                  <line class="m-line" x1="156" y1="80" x2="264" y2="80" marker-end="url(#mL0)"/>
                  <line class="m-line" x1="276" y1="26" x2="384" y2="26" marker-end="url(#mL0)"/>
                  <line class="m-line" x1="276" y1="80" x2="384" y2="80" marker-end="url(#mL0)"/>
                </svg>
                <p class="mini-cap">input enters · output leaves · one boundary</p>
              </figure>
            </div>
          </section>

          <section class="layer-block L1" aria-label="L1 SENSE">
            <h3 class="layer-head"><span class="layer-code">L1</span>SENSE</h3>
            <div class="layer-body">
              <div class="layer-text">
                <p>L1 turns the ambient firehose into durable records you own. Source adapters connect to each source; normalize parses and classifies into one shape; the result is a source record with its provenance attached.</p>
                <p>"Owned" is a property of the record, not a claim on the data: the deployment keeps a local, auditable copy or pointer under your control, with source identity preserved. It is not yet memory — promotion into durable memory happens later, at L2, and only after review.</p>
                <dl class="layer-legend">
                  <div><dt>source adapters</dt><dd>connect and pull each source</dd></div>
                  <div><dt>normalize</dt><dd>parse and classify into one shape</dd></div>
                  <div><dt>source records</dt><dd>user-owned, provenance preserved</dd></div>
                </dl>
              </div>
              <figure class="layer-mini">
                <svg viewBox="0 0 180 150" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
                  <defs><marker id="mL1" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto"><path class="m-h" d="M0,0 L10,5 L0,10 z"/></marker></defs>
                  <rect class="m-box" x="15" y="6" width="150" height="32" rx="6"/><text class="m-t" x="90" y="26" text-anchor="middle">source adapters</text>
                  <line class="m-line" x1="90" y1="38" x2="90" y2="52" marker-end="url(#mL1)"/>
                  <rect class="m-box" x="25" y="54" width="130" height="32" rx="6"/><text class="m-t" x="90" y="74" text-anchor="middle">normalize</text>
                  <line class="m-line" x1="90" y1="86" x2="90" y2="100" marker-end="url(#mL1)"/>
                  <rect class="m-box" x="35" y="102" width="110" height="34" rx="4"/><text class="m-t" x="90" y="123" text-anchor="middle">source records</text>
                </svg>
                <p class="mini-cap">narrowed into records you own</p>
              </figure>
            </div>
          </section>

          <section class="layer-block L2" aria-label="L2 Binding layer">
            <h3 class="layer-head"><span class="layer-code">L2</span>Binding layer</h3>
            <div class="layer-body">
              <div class="layer-text">
                <p>L2 is the durable center, and it keeps memory in strata rather than one blob. The memory SSoT is the git-backed authority — the one record that can accept reviewed changes. Harness memory is a rebuildable serving projection; agent memory is a staged, task-local namespace. Neither is authoritative, and neither can silently promote itself.</p>
                <p>Routing lives here too: the policy router picks mode, model, and tool by sensitivity and task, and converse is a replaceable session harness — not the IO boundary, not an output owner, and not write authority. Collapsing these into one "memory" would hide the anti-lock-in claim and make a vendor cache look authoritative.</p>
                <dl class="layer-legend">
                  <div><dt>memory SSoT</dt><dd>git-backed authority — the record</dd></div>
                  <div><dt>harness memory</dt><dd>rebuildable serving projection</dd></div>
                  <div><dt>agent memory</dt><dd>staged, task-local namespace</dd></div>
                  <div><dt>policy router</dt><dd>mode / model / tool routing</dd></div>
                  <div><dt>converse</dt><dd>replaceable session harness</dd></div>
                </dl>
              </div>
              <figure class="layer-mini">
                <svg viewBox="0 0 200 216" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
                  <defs><marker id="mL2" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto"><path class="m-h" d="M0,0 L10,5 L0,10 z"/></marker></defs>
                  <rect class="m-box" x="25" y="6" width="150" height="28" rx="6"/><text class="m-t" x="100" y="24" text-anchor="middle">policy router</text>
                  <line class="m-line" x1="100" y1="34" x2="100" y2="44" marker-end="url(#mL2)"/>
                  <rect class="m-box m-auth" x="15" y="46" width="170" height="32" rx="6"/><text class="m-t" x="100" y="65" text-anchor="middle">memory SSoT</text>
                  <line class="m-line m-proj" x1="100" y1="78" x2="100" y2="90" marker-end="url(#mL2)"/>
                  <rect class="m-box m-proj" x="30" y="92" width="140" height="26" rx="6"/><text class="m-t" x="100" y="109" text-anchor="middle">harness memory</text>
                  <rect class="m-box m-proj" x="30" y="124" width="140" height="26" rx="6"/><text class="m-t" x="100" y="141" text-anchor="middle">agent memory</text>
                  <line class="m-line" x1="100" y1="150" x2="100" y2="162" marker-end="url(#mL2)"/>
                  <rect class="m-box" x="25" y="164" width="150" height="28" rx="6"/><text class="m-t" x="100" y="182" text-anchor="middle">converse</text>
                </svg>
                <p class="mini-cap">one authority · rebuildable projections</p>
              </figure>
            </div>
          </section>

          <section class="layer-block L3" aria-label="L3 Nested ReAct">
            <h3 class="layer-head"><span class="layer-code">L3</span>Nested ReAct</h3>
            <div class="layer-body">
              <div class="layer-text">
                <p>L3 is the bounded thinking loop, nested inside the larger architecture rather than owning it. It reasons, acts, observes, and reflects, then closes back to reason — a closed cycle with observable exits.</p>
                <p>The load-bearing constraint: "act" emits an intent proposal only. It never reaches the outside world directly; anything with a side effect leaves through L4's gate. The loop can plan, try, and adjust freely, because none of that is a write until the gate says so.</p>
                <dl class="layer-legend">
                  <div><dt>reason</dt><dd>plan the next step</dd></div>
                  <div><dt>act</dt><dd>proposes an intent only</dd></div>
                  <div><dt>observe</dt><dd>read the result / trace</dd></div>
                  <div><dt>reflect</dt><dd>task-local adjustment</dd></div>
                </dl>
              </div>
              <figure class="layer-mini">
                <svg viewBox="0 0 220 156" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
                  <defs><marker id="mL3" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto"><path class="m-h" d="M0,0 L10,5 L0,10 z"/></marker></defs>
                  <rect class="m-box" x="20" y="6" width="180" height="28" rx="6"/><text class="m-t" x="110" y="24" text-anchor="middle">Nested ReAct loop</text>
                  <rect class="m-box" x="26" y="52" width="76" height="32" rx="6"/><text class="m-t" x="64" y="72" text-anchor="middle">reason</text>
                  <rect class="m-box" x="118" y="52" width="76" height="32" rx="6"/><text class="m-t" x="156" y="72" text-anchor="middle">act</text>
                  <rect class="m-box" x="26" y="104" width="76" height="32" rx="6"/><text class="m-t" x="64" y="124" text-anchor="middle">reflect</text>
                  <rect class="m-box" x="118" y="104" width="76" height="32" rx="6"/><text class="m-t" x="156" y="124" text-anchor="middle">observe</text>
                  <line class="m-line" x1="102" y1="68" x2="118" y2="68" marker-end="url(#mL3)"/>
                  <line class="m-line" x1="156" y1="84" x2="156" y2="104" marker-end="url(#mL3)"/>
                  <line class="m-line" x1="118" y1="120" x2="102" y2="120" marker-end="url(#mL3)"/>
                  <line class="m-line" x1="64" y1="104" x2="64" y2="84" marker-end="url(#mL3)"/>
                  <line class="m-line m-proj" x1="194" y1="68" x2="216" y2="68" marker-end="url(#mL3)"/>
                </svg>
                <p class="mini-cap">act proposes; it cannot write</p>
              </figure>
            </div>
          </section>

          <section class="layer-block L4" aria-label="L4 Gated action">
            <h3 class="layer-head"><span class="layer-code">L4</span>Gated action</h3>
            <div class="layer-body">
              <div class="layer-text">
                <p>L4 is the system's one write boundary — the only place a proposed side effect can become a real one. It does not trust the caller: it turns an intent into an explicit, inspectable plan and runs it only if that plan clears the gate.</p>
                <p>An action arrives as a <em>side-effect intent</em> — a request, not permission. It expands into a versioned <em>procedure manifest</em>; the <em>policy check</em> weighs mode, permission, and procedure constraints; then the <em>fail-closed gate</em> defaults to no execution, so a missing manifest, ambiguity, an expired confirmation, a timeout, or an unknown action class all resolve to "do not run" — nothing passes by omission. The outcome is one disposition: <em>approve</em>, <em>refuse</em>, or <em>defer</em> (held safely, unexecuted; resuming re-enters the gate, never a hidden retry).</p>
                <p>Because every harness routes side effects through this one funnel, replacing the conversation harness never rebuilds the write logic, and no harness carries write authority of its own. The safety invariant is a mechanical branch, not a prompt.</p>
                <dl class="layer-legend">
                  <div><dt>side-effect intent</dt><dd>a proposal, not permission</dd></div>
                  <div><dt>procedure manifest</dt><dd>the versioned write plan</dd></div>
                  <div><dt>policy check</dt><dd>mode + permission constraints</dd></div>
                  <div><dt>fail-closed gate</dt><dd>default deny — nothing passes by omission</dd></div>
                  <div><dt>approve / refuse / defer</dt><dd>run, stop, or safely hold</dd></div>
                </dl>
              </div>
              <figure class="layer-mini">
                <svg viewBox="0 0 210 250" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
                  <defs><marker id="mL4" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto"><path class="m-h" d="M0,0 L10,5 L0,10 z"/></marker></defs>
                  <rect class="m-box" x="18" y="6" width="174" height="28" rx="6"/><text class="m-t" x="105" y="24" text-anchor="middle">side-effect intent</text>
                  <line class="m-line" x1="105" y1="34" x2="105" y2="44" marker-end="url(#mL4)"/>
                  <rect class="m-box" x="33" y="46" width="144" height="26" rx="6"/><text class="m-t" x="105" y="63" text-anchor="middle">procedure manifest</text>
                  <line class="m-line" x1="105" y1="72" x2="105" y2="84" marker-end="url(#mL4)"/>
                  <polygon class="m-box" points="105,88 148,110 105,132 62,110"/><text class="m-t" x="105" y="113" text-anchor="middle">policy check</text>
                  <line class="m-line" x1="105" y1="132" x2="105" y2="146" marker-end="url(#mL4)"/>
                  <rect class="m-box" x="45" y="148" width="120" height="36" rx="6"/>
                  <rect class="m-line" x="56" y="160" width="13" height="11" rx="2"/><path class="m-line" d="M58 160 v-3 a4 4 0 0 1 8 0 v3"/>
                  <text class="m-t" x="116" y="170" text-anchor="middle">fail-closed gate</text>
                  <line class="m-line" x1="105" y1="184" x2="105" y2="198" marker-end="url(#mL4)"/>
                  <rect class="m-box" x="24" y="200" width="162" height="28" rx="6"/><text class="m-t" x="105" y="218" text-anchor="middle">approve / refuse / defer</text>
                </svg>
                <p class="mini-cap">the write funnel</p>
              </figure>
            </div>
          </section>

          <section class="layer-block wide L5" aria-label="L5 Governance foundation">
            <h3 class="layer-head"><span class="layer-code">L5</span>Governance foundation</h3>
            <div class="layer-body">
              <div class="layer-text">
                <p>L5 is the meta-loop: not another runtime path, but the reviewed lane that turns operation into improvement. Run traces — conversations, tool calls, procedures, failures — are extracted into candidates and distilled into structured, reviewable artifacts.</p>
                <p>A human review gates any authority change. Approved deltas are promoted into the relevant source of truth; memory reconciliation aligns the SSoT, harness projections, and agent namespaces; apply means a reviewed artifact can change future behavior. The system learns by turning runs into auditable artifacts — recorded as ADR and runbook changes — never by silently mutating an opaque model memory.</p>
                <dl class="layer-legend">
                  <div><dt>run traces</dt><dd>raw evidence from runs</dd></div>
                  <div><dt>extract / distill</dt><dd>candidates into structured artifacts</dd></div>
                  <div><dt>human review</dt><dd>the gate before any authority change</dd></div>
                  <div><dt>promote</dt><dd>land approved deltas into the record</dd></div>
                  <div><dt>memory reconciliation</dt><dd>align SSoT, projections, namespaces</dd></div>
                  <div><dt>apply</dt><dd>a reviewed change alters future behavior</dd></div>
                </dl>
              </div>
              <figure class="layer-mini">
                <svg viewBox="0 0 560 72" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
                  <defs><marker id="mL5" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto"><path class="m-h" d="M0,0 L10,5 L0,10 z"/></marker></defs>
                  <rect class="m-box" x="2" y="22" width="70" height="30" rx="5"/><text class="m-t" x="37" y="41" text-anchor="middle">run traces</text>
                  <line class="m-line" x1="72" y1="37" x2="86" y2="37" marker-end="url(#mL5)"/>
                  <rect class="m-box" x="88" y="22" width="60" height="30" rx="5"/><text class="m-t" x="118" y="41" text-anchor="middle">extract</text>
                  <line class="m-line" x1="148" y1="37" x2="162" y2="37" marker-end="url(#mL5)"/>
                  <rect class="m-box" x="164" y="22" width="58" height="30" rx="5"/><text class="m-t" x="193" y="41" text-anchor="middle">distill</text>
                  <line class="m-line" x1="222" y1="37" x2="236" y2="37" marker-end="url(#mL5)"/>
                  <polygon class="m-box" points="270,20 300,37 270,54 240,37"/><text class="m-t" x="270" y="40" text-anchor="middle">review</text>
                  <line class="m-line" x1="300" y1="37" x2="314" y2="37" marker-end="url(#mL5)"/>
                  <rect class="m-box" x="316" y="22" width="64" height="30" rx="5"/><text class="m-t" x="348" y="41" text-anchor="middle">promote</text>
                  <line class="m-line" x1="380" y1="37" x2="394" y2="37" marker-end="url(#mL5)"/>
                  <rect class="m-box" x="396" y="22" width="76" height="30" rx="5"/><text class="m-t" x="434" y="41" text-anchor="middle">reconcile</text>
                  <line class="m-line" x1="472" y1="37" x2="486" y2="37" marker-end="url(#mL5)"/>
                  <rect class="m-box" x="488" y="22" width="60" height="30" rx="5"/><text class="m-t" x="518" y="41" text-anchor="middle">apply</text>
                </svg>
                <p class="mini-cap">runs → reviewed artifacts → promoted back</p>
              </figure>
            </div>
          </section>
        </div>
        <p class="policy-note">
          The read side runs asynchronously on timers; the conversation harness is
          the interactive loop, not the only process. Reads skip the write gate
          but are still an egress risk, so high-sensitivity reading requires
          mode-aware accessors and leak guards on any path toward a cloud model.
        </p>
        <p class="policy-note">
          There is a second loop above the first: observe runs, extract
          candidates, distill them into artifacts, review by a human, promote
          into the record, reconcile across harnesses, deploy. The assistant
          learns by turning runs into auditable artifacts, not by silently
          mutating an opaque model memory.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="page-section" id="case-examples" aria-labelledby="case-title">
        <div class="section-label">
          <span class="sec-no">04</span>
          <h2 id="case-title">Architecture cuts</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="case-grid">
          <article>
            <header>
              <span>Architecture cut 01</span>
            </header>
            <h3>One write path, fail closed.</h3>
            <p>
              Every assistant-initiated side effect flows through a single gated
              dispatcher. The conversation harness supplies intent and arguments;
              the gate enforces policy. The dispatcher does not depend on how
              many procedures exist, so replacing the harness never means
              rebuilding the write logic, and no harness carries bespoke
              authority of its own.
            </p>
            <p>
              Delivery follows the same shape through its own admission check —
              audience and sensitivity, refusing rather than misrouting — but that
              is a separate boundary from the L4 action gate, not a second copy of
              it.
            </p>
            <blockquote class="case-claim">The safety invariant is a mechanical branch, not a prompt.</blockquote>
          </article>
          <article>
            <header>
              <span>Architecture cut 02</span>
            </header>
            <h3>Memory that stays yours.</h3>
            <p>
              The memory SSoT is a git-backed, reviewable record. Harness and
              agent memory are rebuildable projections and staged namespaces,
              never a second source of truth. A serving layer can be rebuilt or
              replaced without the memory of record moving with it.
            </p>
            <p>
              Because the authority is one reviewed record, no vendor store
              becomes the source of truth, and reconciliation across namespaces
              is a governed action rather than a silent background sync.
            </p>
            <blockquote class="case-claim">One authority, many rebuildable projections.</blockquote>
          </article>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="page-section" id="open-questions" aria-labelledby="questions-title">
        <div class="section-label">
          <span class="sec-no">05</span>
